\begin{frame}{Graphs: Why it works so well}

\begin{itemize}

    \item Our first-order approximation $\varepsilon \theta_1$ punches way above its weight class because:The next phase-drift term ($\varepsilon^4$) is so tiny that even at $t=100$, the phase error hasn't had enough time to build up. The shape-warping term ($\varepsilon^3 \theta_3$) is suppressed by a factor of $1/192$, meaning the true pendulum remains incredibly close to a perfect cosine wave even when swinging at wide angles ($\theta = 0.5 \text{ radians} \approx 28^\circ$)

    \item In asymptotic mathematics, $O(\varepsilon^{-4})$ defines a scale, not an absolute second. The true breakdown happens when the phase error accumulates to an $O(1)$ shift (meaning the waves drift exactly out of phase, peak-to-trough). The actual frequency correction in your solution is $\omega \approx 1 - \frac{\varepsilon^2}{16}$. If we look at the omitted higher-order frequency corrections that we threw away (which would come from the next time scale, $t_2 = \varepsilon^4 t$), the next correction is roughly proportional to $\frac{\varepsilon^4}{3072} t$.  
    
    \item Let's plug in our values ($\varepsilon = 0.5$ and $t = 100$):$$\text{Omitted Phase Error} \approx \frac{(0.5)^4}{3072} \times 100 = \frac{0.0625}{3072} \times 100 \approx 0.002 \text{ radians}$$.
    
    \end{itemize}
\end{frame}


